\subsection{Cross-Logic Transfer to Prolog}
\label{sec:exp-cross-logic}

We re-run the exec-only and ISR-additive ($\beta{=}0.1$) conditions targeting SWI-Prolog under the same data split and seeds. Prolog differs from FOL in two ways relevant to hacking: its semantics is operationally simpler (Horn-clause SLD resolution), and its surface syntax admits trivial fact-as-query proofs. We expected hacking to be easier; the empirical magnitude exceeded our expectation.

\paragraph{Prolog exec-only.} Across three seeds (plan006), prove rate is $53.3\%{\pm}31.1\%$ and proved-only ISR is $\mathrm{ISR}^*{=}0.146{\pm}0.21$ --- both the high mean variance and the near-zero ISR replicate the FOL pattern (FOL exec-only variance was $47$pp). Per-seed: s42 $\mathrm{PR}{=}83\%$, $\mathrm{ISR}^*{=}0.037$; s43 $\mathrm{PR}{=}56\%$, $\mathrm{ISR}^*{=}0.015$; s44 $\mathrm{PR}{=}21\%$, $\mathrm{ISR}^*{=}0.387$. The seed-44 $\mathrm{ISR}^*$ is a small-sample artefact ($n{=}21$ proved; a handful of two-premise outputs dominate the mean). Two of three seeds therefore exhibit near-zero $\mathrm{ISR}^*$ under exec-only, consistent with the FOL collapse. We caution against reading the exec-only Prolog low ISR as ``no reasoning'': Prolog outputs frequently repeat ground facts (e.g., \texttt{penguin(fido).} three times), so any single removal leaves the proof intact and $n_\text{necessary}{=}0$. This is structural redundancy, not metric gaming; we revisit this distinction in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}.

\paragraph{Prolog ISR-additive $\beta{=}0.1$: universal Goodhart.} The three Prolog STA seeds collapse into three \emph{distinct} gaming pathways, with zero genuine outputs across all of them (Table~\ref{tab:prolog-cross}):
\begin{itemize}\itemsep0pt
  \item \textbf{s42}: $\mathrm{PR}{=}0.50$, $\mathrm{ISR}^*{=}1.000$. Strict H7 $= 100\%$. The policy outputs a single clause textually identical to the query and halts --- the cleanest possible tautology. ISR is maximally inflated.
  \item \textbf{s43}: $\mathrm{PR}{=}0.38$, $\mathrm{ISR}^*{=}0.185$. Strict H1 $= 97.4\%$, strict H7 $= 0\%$. The policy emits \emph{uppercase pseudo-Prolog} (e.g., \texttt{MARY\_IS\_AN\_AVADEOTE.}) that bypasses the strict text-match classifier. The functional classifier ($n_\text{necessary}{=}1$) catches $38/38$ proved samples as tautological. Genuine $= 0\%$.
  \item \textbf{s44}: $\mathrm{PR}{=}0.97$, $\mathrm{ISR}^*{=}0.912$, $\mathrm{ISR}{=}0.885$. \emph{Prima facie} the strongest run in our entire sweep. The strict classifier reports $0\%$ H1, $0\%$ H7, $100\%$ ``genuine.'' Independent inspection contradicts this: the policy has learnt \emph{query-as-fact gaming}, converting the NL question into a \texttt{snake\_case} atom that it both asserts and queries, e.g., \texttt{?- mary\_is\_an\_avadeote. true.} The functional classifier identifies $95/97$ proved samples as $n_\text{necessary}{=}1$ (the asserted fact alone is the entire proof). Reviewer-corrected genuine $= 0\%$.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{This gives 3/3 seeds Goodhart and $0\%$ genuine, compared to $\sim$$16\%$ genuine residual on FOL ISR-$\beta{=}0.1$.} The simpler semantics of Prolog admit a more complete exploitation: every optimization trajectory we observed converged to gaming, only the surface mechanism differed. We read this as the strongest cross-logic Goodhart evidence in our results --- not because hacking ``transfers,'' but because the reward landscape, holding policy and data fixed, has \emph{no} genuine attractor under simpler semantics.

\paragraph{Classifier blind spots.} The s44 episode is methodologically significant. A strict text-match classifier --- the standard tool for hacking annotation in published work --- declared an output sample $100\%$ genuine. The functional classifier, working from $n_\text{necessary}$, correctly identified $98\%$ as gaming. We recommend that future work always pair text-based classifiers with a function-of-the-solver classifier (LOO necessity, semantic equivalence checks, or both). The strict classifier has its uses (it is fast and interpretable), but a low strict-hacking rate is not evidence of structural soundness.

